# Check an agent's browser task against the page with Taiko

Clicking `Approve order` takes the browser to a page headed `Session expired`. The click returns without an error, and the page still says `Order 1001 approved`. An agent reports `completed`, but the browser is at `/session-expired`, with none of the order's result markers.

The order site is a local demo. A fixed script stands in for the agent and always reports completion. No LLM is involved. Taiko attempts the action and reads the page. A separate function checks whether the URL and page elements show the required approval result for order `1001`.

## Three outcomes for the same task

<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse">
<thead>
<tr><th scope="col" style="padding:0 1rem 0 0;text-align:left">Case</th><th scope="col" style="padding:0;text-align:left">Verdict</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:0 1rem 0 0">Success</td><td style="padding:0">Accepted</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:0 1rem 0 0">Missing button</td><td style="padding:0">Rejected</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:0 1rem 0 0">Unexpected navigation</td><td style="padding:0">Rejected</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

On success, the browser stays at the expected URL and shows an approved order with its confirmation. If the button is missing, the click throws an error and the order stays visibly `Pending`. Unexpected navigation is easier to miss. The click returns without an error, but the required order result is absent.

## Define what approval requires

For order `1001`, approval requires the expected URL, the requested order ID, the status `Approved`, and the matching confirmation. Result markers are the page elements for those three values. Each set must contain exactly one element, and that element must be visible and have the exact expected text. Approval words elsewhere on the page aren't enough.

~~~javascript
function oneVisibleValue(values, expected) {
  return values.length === 1 &&
    values[0].visible && values[0].text === expected;
}

function isCompleted(observed, expectedURL, input) {
  return observed.url === expectedURL &&
    oneVisibleValue(observed.order, input.orderId) &&
    oneVisibleValue(observed.status, 'Approved') &&
    oneVisibleValue(observed.confirmation,
      `Order ${input.orderId} approved`);
}
~~~

`isCompleted` doesn't read the fake agent's report or the demo's selected scenario. It also rejects duplicate markers, even when their text matches, because the task requires a single order result.

## Keep the click outcome separate

The fake passes `Approve order` to Taiko's [button selector](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/button/) and attempts a [click](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/click/). It records whether the click returned or threw, but reports `completed` in both cases:

~~~javascript
async function optimisticAgent() {
  try {
    await taiko.click(taiko.button('Approve order'),
      { navigationTimeout: 30000 });
    return { report: 'completed', actionResolved: true, error: null };
  } catch (error) {
    return {
      report: 'completed', actionResolved: false,
      error: {
        name: error.name, message: error.message, stack: error.stack },
    };
  }
}
~~~

In `unexpected-navigation.json`, the report is `completed` and `actionResolved` is `true`, but the final URL ends in `/session-expired`. The approval check returns `false` because the expected order result is missing. In the missing-button case, the click error helps explain why the order is still pending.

The missing-button case expects an `Error` with this exact message, including two ordinary spaces before `not`:

~~~text
Button with label Approve order  not found
~~~

The complete runner is `workflow.test.cjs` in the [`taiko-agent-task` folder](https://github.com/tridev-tej/technical-blog-examples/tree/005c62af00ce3cac768dc6e1e5d551fcc246859a/taiko-agent-task) of `technical-blog-examples`. Its `runScenario` function checks the starting page, attempts the action, and then reads the page again for `isCompleted`. The file also contains `readPage` and the assertions for each case.

## Read the page without changing the result

The observer reads the URL with [currentURL](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/currenturl/), the page elements with [evaluate](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/evaluate/), and the number of approval buttons with [elements](https://docs.taiko.dev/api/elementwrapper/). Its callback doesn't add markers or change the status:

~~~javascript
async function observe() {
  const url = await taiko.currentURL();
  const dom = await taiko.evaluate(readPage,
    { navigationTimeout: 30000 });
  const buttons = (
    await taiko.button('Approve order').elements(0, 0)).length;
  return { url, ...dom, buttons };
}
~~~

`isCompleted` uses the result markers. The observer also saves the body text for a weaker check that accepts any occurrence of `Order 1001 approved`. The expired page contains that phrase but none of the markers.

The visibility check is approximate. It uses the element's box dimensions, intersection with the visible browser area, and styles on its ancestor elements. It doesn't prove that the marker is unobscured or accessible.

The observer reads the URL, DOM, and button count one after another. These readings aren't an atomic snapshot: the page can change between them. If your application updates asynchronously, decide when its state has settled enough to read.

## Check the misleading phrase

A phrase search accepts the expired page from the opening:

~~~javascript
function textOnlyCompleted(observed, input) {
  return observed.bodyText.includes(`Order ${input.orderId} approved`);
}
~~~

A control test visits `/session-expired` directly and asserts that `textOnlyCompleted` returns `false`. The function returns `true`, so the assertion raises `AssertionError` with `true !== false`. The control passes only if it captures that expected error. The control fails if there's no error or the error has a different name. A passing control records the phrase search's false positive. The full approval check rejects the same page.

The verdict covers the demo's DOM state, which the success handler changes synchronously. To check that approval was saved, you'd also need evidence from the system that stores it. Authentication, payments, production workflows, frames, shadow DOM, delayed updates, and recovery from broken connections are outside this example.

## Run the example

The runner requires Linux and uses Node's built-in test runner. The recorded setup used Linux x64, Node 22.23.1, npm 10.9.8, Taiko 1.5.0, chrome-remote-interface 0.33.3, and freshly installed bundled Chrome 147.0.7727.117. Both recorded runs passed the same four cases, with no failures or skips. Other platforms and versions haven't been tested.

Use the pinned `technical-blog-examples` revision below and run the example from `taiko-agent-task`. Keep all five reader files together, with the unchanged `package-lock.json` beside `package.json`.

Normal `npm ci` install scripts download the browser, so installation may use the network. Before running the commands, unset every `TAIKO_*` variable, plus `LOCAL_PROTOCOL`, `NODE_OPTIONS`, and `NODE_PATH`. The reader refuses to run with those variables set. The XDG config and cache directories must be new and writable.

Run these commands in order from a directory where you want to clone the repository:

~~~sh
git clone https://github.com/tridev-tej/technical-blog-examples.git
cd technical-blog-examples
git checkout --detach 005c62af00ce3cac768dc6e1e5d551fcc246859a
cd taiko-agent-task
node --version
npm --version
npm ci
npm ls taiko --depth=0
mkdir xdg-config xdg-cache
XDG_CONFIG_HOME="$PWD/xdg-config" \
  XDG_CACHE_HOME="$PWD/xdg-cache" npm test
XDG_CONFIG_HOME="$PWD/xdg-config" \
  XDG_CACHE_HOME="$PWD/xdg-cache" npm test
~~~

Each `npm test` run creates an `evidence-*` directory. Compare `unexpected-navigation.json` with `success.json`: both clicks returned without an error, but only one page has the required order result. The final-page PNGs show what the browser displayed. `text-only-control.json` records the expected failure of the phrase search.

## Check a different task

Take the expected URL and result values from the new task's input, then decide when the page is settled enough to read. Test against a page like `/session-expired`, which has the success words without the required result. Your check should reject that page even when the click returns without an error and the agent reports `completed`.
